# Contributor identity map. Collapses the aliases one person committed under, so
# `git shortlog`, `git log` and `git blame` credit them once instead of splitting the
# total across several names.
#
# Neither upstream ships a .mailmap, so nothing collapses unless it is listed here.
# 01-import-history.sh copies this file into the combined repo as .mailmap, which git
# honours for log, shortlog and blame -- editing it later takes effect immediately and
# needs no history rewrite.
#
# DIRECTION MATTERS. The identity on the LEFT is the canonical one to display; the
# identity on the RIGHT is what actually appears in the commits:
#
#   Canonical Name <canonical@email> <address-found-in-commits>
#   Canonical Name <canonical@email> Name Found In Commits <address-found-in-commits>
#   Canonical Name <canonical@email>          # renames the author of that one address
#
# Getting it backwards is silent: the entry matches nothing and the credit list looks
# untouched. 04-history-report.sh probes every entry and reports the ones with no
# effect, so re-run it after editing.
#
# The addresses below come from 04's own output. Which address is canonical is a
# convention, not a fact: for third parties this uses the most recent address seen in
# the history, on the assumption it is the one still in use.
#
# Bot identities (dependabot[bot], github-actions[bot]) are left alone: they are
# accurate as they stand, and collapsing them would misattribute automated commits.

# The gmail line also covers the "onobc" display name, since a two-part entry matches
# on address alone regardless of the name on the commit.
Chris Bono <onobc@apache.org> <cbono@vmware.com>
Chris Bono <onobc@apache.org> <chris.bono@gmail.com>

Soby Chacko <soby.chacko@broadcom.com> <chackos@vmware.com>
